{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FFRcVfebVhe4ZyHEzNdSEz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"GKR input evaluation doesn't use NEEDS_SHMEM. Good. So the PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS flag only affects the Round 0 kernels (zerocheck and logup).\n\nOK, let me step back and think about what to actually do. The current measurements from the vpmm-bulk-page-creation state are solid. The batch-round0-descriptors commit on top is broken (causes 12+ min regression at APC 300). The correct course of action is:\n\n1. Revert the batch-round0-descriptors commit \n2. Either fix and re-apply it, or abandon it and try a different approach\n \nRound 0 is clearly the bottleneck with no scaling from APC 0 to 300, and the batch approach itself is sound—the CUDA kernels and FFI integration work fine. The issue is likely in how I'm constructing the symbolic constraints from the verification key on line 255 of round0_batched.rs, which might be doing something expensive that wasn't necessary before.\n\nThough the per-AIR overhead from the precompute report suggests only ~50ms total for 500 AIRs, so that's not explaining the 12-minute regression. I'm wondering if the real problem is downstream—maybe after the batch phase filters out the batchable AIRs, the multi-stream Phase 2 ends up with severely imbalanced work across threads, causing some threads to stall while others finish quickly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":420,"cache_read_input_tokens":164171,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":420},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"694fb196-69bb-4e1f-a777-5a160503c446","uuid":"620913aa-f638-4bd6-ae8d-0d77f1d24067"}
