ed setup cost.\n\nBut looking at the actual code, the work items already have most of what we need. The real bottleneck in `process_air_round0` is setting up the NTT evaluation context and calling `evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu`, which itself builds the eval context from scratch each time.\n\nI tried precomputing the logup rules earlier and it only saved ~5ms, so the per-AIR CPU overhead is actually pretty small. That 1.8ms figure might be inflated by GPU driver overhead rather than pure CPU work.\n\nLet me think differently—what if I overlap Round 0 with the CPU work that happens after GKR evaluation completes, like transcript operations and challenge sampling?\n\nLooking at the actual code flow, after Round 0 finishes on the GPU there's CPU-side polynomial construction that could be a bottleneck. The s_0 computation involves DFT operations that might be worth overlapping with early MLE round work on the GPU.\n\nI need to stop second-guessing myself and commit to the most straightforward optimization.\n\nI'm ranking three concrete approaches: reverting the batch-round0-descriptors change and fixing the CPU overhead it introduced (likely responsible for the 12+ minute regression), overlapping the s_0 polynomial interpolation with MLE GPU work to save 10-30ms, or fusing the zerocheck and logup Round 0 kernels into a single kernel per AIR to reduce kernel launch overhead and trace data reads.\n\nLooking more closely at idea 3, I'm realizing the kernel consolidation potential is significant—each AIR currently launches four separate kernels in Round 0, and with roughly 312 AIRs per batch, that's over a thousand kernel launches I could potentially halve by merging the zerocheck and logup evaluation into one kernel that writes both result sets.\n\nThe memory bandwidth savings from reading trace data once instead of twice could yield around 10ms of wall-clock improvement, though that's still modest. But I'm thinking bigger now—what if I could terminate the per-trace evaluation early in the MLE rounds?\n\nAt APC 300, the 623 AIR instances mean more work per round, especially since many traces are small and become non-foldable after a few rounds. Instead of iterating through all traces and skipping the ones that are done, I could partition them into active and completed sets, only processing the active ones as the rounds progress. This should cut the per-round overhead significantly as traces get folded down, potentially saving 20-40ms at APC 300 with moderate complexity.\n\nBut the more practical move right now is to revert the broken commit and fix the batch Round 0 approach instead. The kernels themselves are solid from previous validation, and getting Round 0 working properly could yield 25-50ms of improvement, which is substantial. I'll frame this as fixing and validating the batch Round 0 descriptor arrays.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1384,"cache_read_input_tokens":166626,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1384},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"694fb196-69bb-4e1f-a777-5a160503c446","uuid":"a49bf2e1-a0d0-421e-bfc9-82c241c0eef2"}
