{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016GVDeMEogCdPo2VFxGYcA4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Goal\n2\t\n3\tFix the batch Round 0 descriptor-array optimization so it works at APC 300 without the 12+ minute regression introduced by commit 0d1bb2f9. Round 0 is the worst-scaling component of STARK excl trace: 177ms at APC 0 vs 187ms at APC 300 (0.95x, no scaling despite 2.35x fewer cells). The batched approach, once working, should save 25-50ms at APC 300 by eliminating ~500 per-AIR kernel launches for small AIRs, while preserving the multi-stream path for large AIRs.\n4\t\n5\t## Current Code Path\n6\t\n7\t### Round 0 evaluation flow (mod.rs, `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`)\n8\t\n9\t1. **Phase 1** (single-threaded, lines 900-1190): Build work items, compute eq_xis, prepare selectors, allocate d_batch_array.\n10\t\n11\t2. **Phase 1b** (single-threaded, lines 1194-1210): The batch path from commit 0d1bb2f9:\n12\t   - `identify_batchable_airs()` (round0_batched.rs:42) — marks AIRs with coset-parallel mode and intermediates < 16M Fp as batchable; requires >= 50 qualifying AIRs.\n13\t   - `batch_round0_small_airs()` (round0_batched.rs:192) — for each batchable AIR, calls `build_logup_rules_for_air()` (round0_batched.rs:96), which:\n14\t     - Calls `SymbolicConstraints::from(&single_pk.vk.symbolic_constraints)` to convert compact VK representation to full expression trees\n15\t     - Builds a `SymbolicDagBuilder` and DAG\n16\t     - Creates `SymbolicRulesGpu::new(&dag, true)` — computes rules and buffer sizes\n17\t     - Computes numer/denom weights\n18\t     - Uploads rules and weights to GPU (3 H2D transfers per AIR)\n19\t   - Groups by num_cosets, launches batched zerocheck and logup kernels\n20\t   - Runs per-AIR extraction (writes to d_batch_array via GPU kernels)\n21\t\n22\t3. **Phase 2** (multi-threaded, lines 1212-1400): For each non-batchable AIR, calls `process_air_round0()` (mod.rs:234), which:\n23\t   - Also calls `SymbolicConstraints::from()` per AIR (line 241)\n24\t   - Builds the same DAG and rules in `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu()` (round0.rs:162)\n25\t   - Launches per-AIR zerocheck + logup kernels\n26\t   - Runs per-AIR GPU extraction\n27\t\n28\t### Root cause of the regression\n29\t\n30\tThe 12+ minute APC 300 hang is caused by `batch_round0_small_airs()` running single-threaded on ~300 batchable AIRs. The per-AIR work includes `SymbolicConstraints::from()`, DAG construction, rule compilation, weight computation, and 3 device uploads. While each operation is individually fast (~0.1-1ms), the total for ~300 AIRs is significant. Additionally, the batched CUDA kernels themselves may be hanging for certain AIR configurations (this must be diagnosed first — see Step 1 below).\n31\t\n32\tNote: The non-batched path performs the same per-AIR work in `process_air_round0`, but distributes it across 8 multi-stream threads. The batch path concentrates all work on the main thread.\n33\t\n34\t### Why the same per-AIR work is done twice\n35\t\n36\tBoth the batch path (`build_logup_rules_for_air`) and the non-batched path (`evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu`) independently build SymbolicDagBuilder, SymbolicRulesGpu, and compute numer/denom weights from scratch. Neither reuses the other's output. The non-batched path does NOT store its computed rules anywhere reusable — it builds them, launches the kernel, and discards them. The `AirDataGpu.logup_round0_buffer_size` field (pkey.rs:35) stores only the buffer size, not the full rules.\n37\t\n38\t## Changes\n39\t\n40\t### Step 0: Diagnose the exact hang cause\n41\t\n42\tBefore writing any fix, determine whether the 12+ minute regression is caused by:\n43\t**(a)** CPU-side work in `batch_round0_small_airs` (DAG/rule construction for ~300 AIRs), or\n44\t**(b)** The batched CUDA kernel itself hanging (infinite loop or extremely slow execution)\n45\t\n46\t**Diagnostic approach:** Add a `tracing::info!` timestamp after `batch_round0_small_airs` returns and before Phase 2 starts. Rebuild and run APC 300 with the batch commit. If the info message appears quickly (~1 second), the hang is in the GPU kernel. If it never appears within 60 seconds, the hang is in CPU-side work.\n47\t\n48\tIf **(a)**: The fix below (hoisting DAG/rule construction to Phase 1) will resolve it.\n49\tIf **(b)**: Investigate the batched kernel launch parameters (`total_blocks`, `block_x`, `intermediates_size`) for APC 300. Check for excessively large grids, out-of-bounds memory access, or missing termination conditions. Use `compute-sanitizer` if needed.\n50\t\n51\t### Step 1: Revert commit 0d1bb2f9\n52\t\n53\t- File: git operation\n54\t- Action: `git revert 0d1bb2f9` to cleanly revert \"Batch Round 0 descriptor arrays with PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS\"\n55\t- This restores the vpmm-bulk-page-creation state (last known working state)\n56\t- Verify: APC 0 and APC 300 benchmarks produce correct proofs with expected timing (~1806ms / ~1123ms STARK excl trace)\n57\t\n58\t### Step 2: Re-apply PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS separately\n59\t\n60\t- File: `crates/cuda-backend/build.rs`\n61\t- Action: Add `.flag(\"-DPRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS\")` to the cc::Build chain (line 26 in current code)\n62\t- File: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/utils.cuh`\n63\t- Action: Add the `#ifdef PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS` guard for `DISPATCH_BOOL_PAIR` and `DEFINE_DISPATCH_N_B1_B2` macros (same changes as in commit 0d1bb2f9, lines 50-76)\n64\t- Why: Reduces cubin from ~9.3M to ~6.5M, which was needed to avoid GPU OOM at APC 100/300 after CUDA rebuilds. Safe because l_skip=4 gives skip_domain=16 <= WARP_SIZE=32.\n65\t- Verify: APC 0 and APC 300 benchmarks still produce correct proofs with same timing\n66\t\n67\t### Step 3: Hoist logup rule construction from per-AIR to Phase 1\n68\t\n69\tInstead of building logup rules inside `batch_round0_small_airs` (single-threaded for ~300 AIRs) or inside `process_air_round0` (per-AIR in Phase 2), pre-build them once during Phase 1 work-item preparation.\n70\t\n71\t- File: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n72\t- Action: Add a `logup_rules: Option<LogupRulesOnDevice>` field to `Round0AirWorkItem` (line 209-232). Define `LogupRulesOnDevice` as:\n73\t  ```rust\n74\t  pub(super) struct LogupRulesOnDevice {\n75\t      pub d_rules: DeviceBuffer<u64>,\n76\t      pub rules_len: usize,\n77\t      pub buffer_size: u32,\n78\t      pub d_numer_weights: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n79\t      pub d_denom_weights: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n80\t      pub denom_sum_init: EF,\n81\t  }\n82\t  ```\n83\t- Action: During Phase 1 work-item construction (the loop at ~lines 1000-1100), after computing `eq_3bs`, build the logup rules for each AIR:\n84\t  ```rust\n85\t  let logup_rules = if !eq_3bs.is_empty() {\n86\t      let single_air_constraints = SymbolicConstraints::from(&single_pk.vk.symbolic_constraints);\n87\t      Some(build_logup_rules_on_device(&single_air_constraints, &beta_pows, &eq_3bs)?)\n88\t  } else {\n89\t      None\n90\t  };\n91\t  ```\n92\t  Store in the work item.\n93\t- Why: Phase 1 is already single-threaded and builds per-AIR data (eq_xis, selectors, lambda_pows). Adding logup rules here avoids redundant computation in both the batch path and the per-AIR path.\n94\t\n95\t### Step 4: Modify `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` to accept precomputed rules\n96\t\n97\t- File: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0.rs`\n98\t- Action: Change `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` to accept an optional `precomputed_rules: Option<&LogupRulesOnDevice>` parameter. When `Some`, skip DAG construction (lines 193-239) and use the precomputed rules directly. When `None`, fall back to the current behavior.\n99\t- Action: Update `process_air_round0` in `mod.rs` to pass `w.logup_rules.as_ref()`.\n100\t- Why: This eliminates per-AIR DAG construction from the multi-threaded Phase 2, saving ~0.1ms × 39 AIRs per thread = ~4ms per thread.\n101\t\n102\t### Step 5: Re-implement batch path using precomputed rules\n103\t\n104\t- File: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0_batched.rs`\n105\t- Action: Replace `build_logup_rules_for_air()` in `batch_round0_small_airs()` (lines 255-259) with reading from the precomputed `w.logup_rules` field:\n106\t  ```rust\n107\t  let logup_rules = w.logup_rules.as_ref(); // Already built in Phase 1\n108\t  ```\n109\t- Action: Update `BatchAirDesc` to store references to the precomputed device buffers (`d_rules`, `d_numer_weights`, `d_denom_weights`) instead of owned copies.\n110\t- Action: Remove the `build_logup_rules_for_air()` function entirely.\n111\t- Why: Eliminates all redundant CPU-side DAG construction and device uploads from the batch path.\n112\t\n113\t### Step 6: Add the `SymbolicConstraints::from` call once in Phase 1\n114\t\n115\t- File: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n116\t- Action: Store the `SymbolicConstraints` in the work item (add field `symbolic: SymbolicConstraints<F>` to `Round0AirWorkItem`). Compute it once in Phase 1 and reuse in both the batch path (via logup_rules) and the non-batched path.\n117\t- Why: Currently `SymbolicConstraints::from()` is called per AIR in both `process_air_round0` (line 241) and `batch_round0_small_airs` (line 255). Hoisting it to Phase 1 ensures single computation.\n118\t\n119\t## Invariants\n120\t\n121\t1. **Correctness**: The logup rules (DAG, encoded rules, numer/denom weights) must be bit-identical whether computed in Phase 1 or per-AIR. The DAG construction is deterministic given the same `VkSymbolicConstraints`, `beta_pows`, and `eq_3bs` inputs.\n122\t\n123\t2. **Device pointer lifetime**: The `LogupRulesOnDevice` device buffers must live as long as the batch kernel or per-AIR kernel that references them. Since they are stored in `Round0AirWorkItem` which lives for the entire scope of `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`, this is guaranteed.\n124\t\n125\t3. **Batch/non-batch equivalence**: The batch path and per-AIR path must produce identical results in `d_batch_array`. This is testable by running with the batch threshold set to 0 (force all AIRs through non-batched path) and comparing metrics.\n126\t\n127\t4. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (~20 AIRs per segment), the batch path is inactive (< 50 qualifying AIRs). The only change is that logup rules are precomputed in Phase 1 instead of per-AIR in Phase 2. Since Phase 1 is single-threaded in both cases, the overhead is the same.\n128\t\n129\t5. **PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS safety**: With l_skip=4, skip_domain=16 <= WARP_SIZE=32, the NEEDS_SHMEM=true variants are never dispatched. This applies to both batched and per-AIR kernels.\n130\t\n131\t## Measurement Plan\n132\t\n133\t1. After Step 1 (revert): Run full benchmark suite (APC 0, 100, 300). Verify results match pre-batch-round0 state (~1806ms / ~1301ms / ~1123ms).\n134\t\n135\t2. After Step 2 (PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS): Run full benchmark suite. Verify no regression.\n136\t\n137\t3. After Steps 3-6 (re-implemented batch path): Run full benchmark suite.\n138\t   - **APC 0**: Expect no regression (batch path inactive). STARK excl trace ~1806ms.\n139\t   - **APC 300**: Expect Round 0 improvement of 25-50ms (187ms → ~140-160ms). STARK excl trace ~1073-1098ms.\n140\t   - **APC 100**: Expect Round 0 improvement of 15-30ms.\n141\t\n142\t4. Run `cargo nextest run -p openvm-cuda-backend --test-threads=4` to verify all tests pass.\n143\t\n144\t5. Run nsight profile on APC 300 to verify kernel launch count reduction (expect ~500 fewer launches per segment).\n145\t\n146\t## Rollback Criteria\n147\t\n148\t- If APC 300 Round 0 does not improve by at least 15ms (below rollback threshold): revert Steps 3-6, keep Steps 1-2.\n149\t- If APC 0 regresses by more than 20ms: revert all changes.\n150\t- If the diagnostic in Step 0 reveals the hang is in the CUDA kernel (not CPU), the kernel must be debugged separately before re-applying the batch path. In that case, stop after Step 2 and document the kernel bug.\n151\t- If the PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS flag (Step 2) alone causes any benchmark regression or test failure: do not apply it; document the failure and proceed with Steps 3-6 without it.\n152\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JPA4RSMeQ3tv79eo7vUE4f","session_id":"694fb196-69bb-4e1f-a777-5a160503c446","uuid":"742405c1-a66a-4d07-a387-4d3157dd1736","timestamp":"2026-04-15T14:19:50.872Z"}
